          "model_answer": "One primary reason why young British men volunteered in such massive numbers in the autumn of 1914 was genuine moral outrage over the German invasion of neutral Belgium [2.1]. Under the 1839 Treaty of London, Britain had promised to protect Belgian neutrality. When the German army invaded and dismissed the treaty as a \"scrap of paper\", the British public viewed the war as an honourable moral crusade to defend international law. Posters like \"The Scrap of Paper\" and newspaper reports of civilian suffering in Louvain convinced thousands of men that it was their sacred duty to enlist [2.2]. Therefore, this powerful sense of moral indignation directly caused young British men to volunteer in such massive numbers in the autumn of 1914.\\n\\nAnother decisive reason explaining why young British men volunteered in such massive numbers in the autumn of 1914 was the creation of \"Pals Battalions\" [3.1]. War Secretary Lord Kitchener allowed friends, workmates, and sports teams to enlist and serve side-by-side. In towns across Hampshire, dockyard workers and clerks joined the \"Pompey Pals\" together as a single community [3.2]. Enlisting with friends removed the fear of isolation and turned military service into an exciting shared adventure, while creating strong peer loyalty where no one wanted to let down their mates. Consequently, local civic pride and intense peer solidarity ensured that young British men volunteered in such massive numbers in the autumn of 1914.\\n\\nA third major factor why young British men volunteered in such massive numbers in the autumn of 1914 was a mixture of economic necessity and unbearable social pressure [4.1, 5.1]. In 1914, millions lived in poverty with no welfare state. The army offered guaranteed wages through the \"King’s Shilling\", three warm meals a day, new boots, and separation allowances for families [4.2]. At the same time, propaganda posters like \"Women of Britain Say GO!\" and civilian groups handing out white feathers publicly shamed men who stayed behind as cowards [5.2]. As a result, escaping factory poverty alongside the desperate fear of public disgrace compelled young British men to volunteer in such massive numbers in the autumn of 1914.",
