          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The Accrington Pals (11th East Lancashire Regiment) and the Leeds Pals (15th West Yorkshire Regiment).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The guarantee that men could serve, train, and fight alongside their brothers, neighbors, and coworkers made signing up socially irresistible. However, when these units advanced together against German machine-gun lines on the Western Front, hundreds of men from the exact same street or factory were wiped out in minutes, concentrating unimaginable grief and trauma on tight-knit industrial communities back home.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> On 1 July 1916, the British Army suffered 57,470 casualties (19,240 killed) in a single morning. Units like the Accrington Pals lost over 700 of their 1,000 men in twenty minutes. The catastrophic civilian shockwave—where entire towns were plunged into mourning overnight—shattered the concept of localized recruiting, forcing the War Office to abandon Pals Battalions in favor of anonymous national conscription.</li></ol>"
