          "model_answer": "One primary reason why the First Day of the Somme resulted in catastrophic British casualties was the failure of the preliminary artillery bombardment to destroy German defenses [4.1]. General Haig’s tactical plan relied on a seven-day barrage of 1.5 million shells to obliterate enemy trenches and shred the barbed wire guarding No Man’s Land. However, approximately one-third of the shells were poorly made duds that failed to explode, while shrapnel shells could not cut dense 30-foot wire entanglements [4.2]. When British soldiers advanced, they found the wire barriers completely intact and were trapped in concentrated fields of fire. Therefore, the failure of the artillery to clear enemy wire directly caused the First Day of the Somme to result in catastrophic British casualties.\\n\\nAnother crucial reason why the First Day of the Somme resulted in catastrophic British casualties was the superior protection provided by German underground fortifications [2.2]. German soldiers sheltered up to forty feet below ground in reinforced concrete dugouts (Stollen), surviving the week-long artillery bombardment virtually unharmed. The moment the British barrage lifted, German machine-gun crews sprinted to the surface, set up their Spandau Maxim guns, and fired 500 rounds per minute into the advancing infantry [4.2]. British soldiers had no cover and no radio communications to call for artillery support. Consequently, entrenched German machine gunners opening fire at point-blank range ensured that the First Day of the Somme resulted in catastrophic British casualties.\\n\\nA third significant factor explaining why the First Day of the Somme resulted in catastrophic British casualties was the rigid and inflexible infantry tactics ordered by British commanders [4.1]. Inexperienced soldiers were ordered to advance in slow, straight, shoulder-to-shoulder lines carrying 30kg packs, having been wrongly assured that no Germans were left alive. Because frontline telephone lines were quickly severed by shellfire, commanders far behind the lines could not adjust orders, sending wave after wave of men into the same fatal kill-zones [4.2]. As a result, walking in rigid formations without tactical flexibility meant that the First Day of the Somme resulted in catastrophic British casualties.",
