          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[00:45] Recall:</strong> By late 1914, how many continuous miles did the Western Front trench line stretch from the Belgian coast to the Swiss border?</li><li><strong>[01:40] Explain:</strong> Explain why defensive weapons (machine guns firing 500 rounds per minute and heavy artillery) completely dominated early 20th-century warfare over offensive infantry tactics.</li><li><strong>[02:30] Challenge:</strong> Historiographical Evaluation: Was Field Marshal Douglas Haig a callous \"Butcher of the Somme\" who wasted lives, or an innovative general learning how to fight the first industrial war?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Over 400 continuous miles (approx. 700 km).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> A single Vickers or Spandau machine gun crew of four men behind sandbags could unleash the firepower of 40 riflemen, cutting down hundreds of advancing infantry caught in uncut barbed wire. Artillery shells caused 60% of all battlefield casualties, pulverizing drainage systems into mud that swallowed soldiers and tanks alike.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Post-war revisionists branded Haig a \"donkey leading lions\" who repeated suicidal frontal assaults in the face of machine guns. However, modern historians (such as Gary Sheffield) argue Haig faced a technological stalemate nobody had ever encountered. By 1918, Haig pioneered the \"all-arms\" modern combat doctrine—combining tanks, creeping artillery barrages, aerial reconnaissance, and stormtrooper tactics—that successfully broke the Hindenburg Line and won the war.</li></ol>"
