          "teacher_guidance": "Jeremy Paxman analyzes the Defense of the Realm Act (DORA, August 1914): press censorship, watering down beer, rationing, women in munitions factories (\"Canary Girls\"), and the expansion of state power. Recommended Watch Window: 00:30–04:30 • Lesson Phase: Guided Instruction & The Total War State. Tone Alert: Sharp, engaging BBC historical documentary.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:00] Recall:</strong> What sweeping emergency legislation passed on 8 August 1914 gave the British government unprecedented control over citizens' daily lives, property, and civil liberties?</li><li><strong>[02:15] Explain:</strong> Why were female munitions workers who filled artillery shells with TNT called \"Canary Girls\", and what severe health risks did they endure for the war effort?</li><li><strong>[03:45] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate how the First World War permanently expanded the power of the British state over the individual: why could society never return to Victorian laissez-faire?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The Defence of the Realm Act (DORA).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Exposure to toxic trinitrotoluene (TNT) caused toxic jaundice that turned their skin and hair bright yellow (earning the nickname \"Canary Girls\"), as well as causing severe liver failure, respiratory damage, and premature childbirth. Despite this, over 700,000 women risked explosions and poisoning to produce 80% of Britain's shells.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Prior to 1914, the British government had minimal daily intervention in ordinary lives (no ID cards, low income tax, free press). DORA introduced daylight saving time, British Summer Time, pub licensing hours, food rationing cards, state-controlled railways, and industrial conscription. Total war demonstrated that national survival required centralized government planning, establishing the administrative template for the modern welfare state.</li></ol>"
