          "model_answer": "The transition from the Shell Crisis of 1915 to the introduction of compulsory conscription in 1916 represented a profound shift from voluntary civilian cooperation to centralized state control [2.1, 3.1].\\n\\nThe crisis began in May 1915 following the failed British attack at Aubers Ridge. Commander-in-Chief Sir John French leaked reports to the Daily Mail revealing that British troops were being slaughtered because the army had run out of high-explosive artillery shells, with guns rationed to just four rounds per day [2.1]. The resulting public outrage forced Prime Minister H.H. Asquith to dissolve his Liberal administration and create a wartime coalition government. In response, David Lloyd George was appointed to head the newly established Ministry of Munitions, seizing control of private factories and deploying women into engineering to nationalize shell production [2.2].\\n\\nHowever, solving the ammunition shortage immediately exposed an even more desperate crisis: an acute shortage of military manpower [3.1]. By late 1915, the voluntary recruitment system had exhausted itself. Massive casualties at Gallipoli and the Battle of Loos, combined with growing public awareness of the horrors of trench warfare, led voluntary enlistment to collapse. The War Office needed 50,000 fresh men every month to prepare for the upcoming Somme offensive, yet voluntary appeals and the Lord Derby Scheme failed to meet recruitment targets [3.1].\\n\\nConsequently, on 27 January 1916, Parliament took the unprecedented step of passing the Military Service Act [3.1]. This legislation abolished Britain’s historic volunteer tradition, imposing compulsory military conscription on all unmarried men aged 18 to 41. Thus, the supply emergency of the Shell Crisis led directly to the total state mobilization of British manpower.",
