          "model_answer": "On one hand, there is strong evidence to agree that the Treaty of Versailles was so harsh that it made another European war inevitable [3.1, 4.1]. In particular, Article 231 (the \"War Guilt Clause\") forced Germany to accept sole blame for starting the war, which caused deep national humiliation across Germany. Furthermore, setting reparations at £6.6 billion in 1921 stripped Germany of money and triggered catastrophic economic chaos, including the 1923 hyperinflation crisis. Right-wing politicians like Adolf Hitler used these bitter grievances to attack the Weimar democracy and promise military revenge, which strongly supports the view that the Treaty of Versailles was so harsh that it made another European war inevitable.\\n\\nOn the other hand, many historians disagree that the Treaty of Versailles was so harsh that it made another European war inevitable [5.2]. As historian Margaret MacMillan points out, the treaty was far less punitive than the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, which Germany had forced upon Russia in 1918. Germany also remained the most populous and industrially powerful country in Central Europe, with its coalfields, factories, and transport networks completely undamaged by fighting. In addition, Germany recovered strongly in the late 1920s under Gustav Stresemann, proving that the Treaty of Versailles was not so harsh that it made another European war inevitable.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while the treaty created dangerous anger, I disagree that the Treaty of Versailles was so harsh that it made another European war inevitable on its own [5.2]. The decisive reason for conflict was not the wording of the treaty in 1919, but the total failure of Britain and France to enforce its terms during the 1930s. When Adolf Hitler began rearming Germany and remilitarised the Rhineland in 1936, the Allies chose appeasement rather than collective action. Therefore, it was the weakness of Allied enforcement rather than the terms of 1919 that led to conflict, disproving the claim that the Treaty of Versailles was so harsh that it made another European war inevitable.",
